  "result": "I've fixed it: values in a nested `default_map` now apply to subcommands. The new test fails on the old code and passes with the fix, and the full test suite passes (1907 passed, 102 skipped, 1 expected failure).\n\n**What changed:**\n- **`src/click/core.py:392`:** a subcommand used to get the parent's whole `default_map`. It now gets only `parent.default_map.get(info_name)`, the entry under its own name. If that entry is `UNSET`, it's treated as missing.\n- **`tests/test_defaults.py`:** added `test_default_map_subcommands`. It checks four cases:\n  - a direct subcommand (`runserver` gets `port=5000`);\n  - a nested group (`db migrate` gets its `url`);\n  - a subcommand with no entry, which falls back to the option's own default;\n  - an entry set to `UNSET`, which also falls back.\n- **`CHANGES.md`:** added a 8.5.1 (unreleased) section with an entry for this fix.\n\nThe fix also changes how a subcommand without its own `default_map` entry behaves. It used to pick up top-level keys from the parent's map, which only worked by accident. It now gets no defaults from the map. `docs/commands.md` already documents the nested form, so that's the intended behaviour, but any code that relied on the old flattening would change.\n\nNothing is committed yet.",
